    "result_text": "All 55 tests pass with **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the structured address form for `POST /api/owners`, keeping the flat `address` input working for backward compatibility.\n\n**API contract (`openapi.yml`)**\n- Added optional `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` to the `OwnerFields` schema (inherited by `Owner`, so they appear on both request and response).\n- Removed `address` from the required list \u2014 an owner no longer must send flat `address` at the schema level (`city` stays required, so a missing city is still a 400 from bean validation). Address-presence is now enforced in the controller.\n- Documented `address` as the composed, normalized output value.\n\n**Domain model (`Owner.java`)**\n- Added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` columns and accessors. MapStruct auto-maps them in both directions (verified in the generated `OwnerMapperImpl`).\n\n**Database schemas** \u2014 added `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns to the hsqldb (used by tests), h2, mysql, and postgres schema files.\n\n**Controller (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`)** \u2014 replaced the flat-address block with a new `applyAddress(owner)` helper that:\n- Prefers the structured fields: when `addressLine1` is non-blank, it normalizes both lines (existing `normalizeAddress`: trim, collapse whitespace, upper-case, expand `ST\u2192STREET` etc.) and composes `address` = normalized `addressLine1`, plus a single space and normalized `addressLine2` when present.\n- Falls back to the normalized flat `address` otherwise.\n- Rejects a request (400) when neither form supplies a non-blank address.\n\nThe household hash, postcode validation, and locality already read the shared top-level `city`/`postcode` fields, which are common to both forms \u2014 the tests (Cp29/30/36) confirm these continue to work with structured owners.\n\nThe new/changed acceptance tests (Cp01, Cp12, Cp29, Cp30, Cp36, Cp44) and all pre-existing tests are green.",
